v 3
d applyvarlabels: apply variable labels to the data in memory from a two-column (Variable, Label) crosswalk
d
d Label the variables in memory from an Excel workbook or a delimited file
d that lists each variable name beside the label it should carry.  Solves the
d Statalist problem (thread 1768839) where a levelsof/tokenize loop puts the
d wrong label on the wrong variable because levelsof sorts the labels
d alphabetically.  applyvarlabels reads the crosswalk in row order and matches
d every label to its variable BY NAME, never by position.  It writes a
d capture-guarded relabel do-file (a variable named in the crosswalk but absent
d from the data is skipped, not an error), runs it to apply the labels, and
d prints an inventory of what did not line up: crosswalk rows with no such
d variable, and variables in memory with no crosswalk row.  The data in memory
d are never sorted, dropped, or reordered - the crosswalk is read in its own
d frame, so nothing changes but the variable labels themselves.  The generated
d do-file is a keepable, rerunnable artifact (see dofile()).  Reads .xlsx/.xls
d and .csv/.tsv/.txt; nocase matches names ignoring case; norun writes the
d relabel do-file without applying it.
d
d Requires: Stata 16.0 or newer
d
d Author: Eric A. Booth, Sr Researcher, Texas 2036
d Support: eric.a.booth@gmail.com
d
d Distribution-Date: 20260718
d
f applyvarlabels.ado
f applyvarlabels.sthlp
f example_applyvarlabels.do
f example_varlabels.xlsx
f make_example_varlabels.py
